    "created_at": "2021-08-30T17:25:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-08-30T17:25:22Z",
    "body": "Are the two solutions really mutually exclusive?\r\n\r\n- `content` attribute is a widely known solution especially among those who don't study HTML on volatile specs (see https://github.com/schemaorg/schemaorg/issues/2937). It mirrors the solution used for `<meta>` element. It is only a matter of counting occurrences in the wild (i.e. hoping the illness hasn't spread too much)\r\n- on the other hand Microdata standard's point of strength was to rely upon specific HTML attributes to build a consistent Structured Data framework. The idea behind structured data is to use human-readable content whenever necessary, so a `<data>` element with a `value` attribute should be the only sensible situation for using a machine-readable equivalent (apart from `<meta>` - which is reserved for data not relevant to users). Not to mention that `<data>` might have other interesting uses, but its usefulness is severely crippled by not being usable for either Microdata or RDFa.\r\n\r\n`<data@value>` has always been part of both \"HTML5\" features and Microdata specs (also in W3 flavor). If Google search doesn't read it, that's \"their\" fault not to have followed specs carefully, isn't it?",
    "author_association": "NONE",
